CAT = "cdcfibSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
# Fire safety, first aid and emergency response: the basics every officer of the four services learns.
Q = [
("The three things a fire needs, known as the fire triangle, are:",
 "Heat, fuel and oxygen", ["Heat, smoke and water", "Fuel, wind and sparks", "Oxygen, ash and light"],
 "Take away any one side of the triangle and the fire goes out: cooling removes heat, starving removes fuel and smothering removes oxygen."),
("Covering a burning pot with a damp cloth or fire blanket puts the fire out by:",
 "Cutting off the oxygen", ["Cooling the fuel", "Removing the fuel", "Breaking the flame chain"],
 "Smothering stops air reaching the flames. Lifting the cover too soon lets oxygen back in and the fire can flare up again."),
("Why must you never throw water on a burning pan of cooking oil?",
 "It makes the oil explode outward", ["It cools the oil too slowly", "It makes thick black smoke", "It turns into poisonous gas"],
 "Water sinks into the hot oil, flashes to steam and throws burning oil out of the pan. Turn off the heat and smother it with a lid or fire blanket."),
("Which extinguisher is safe on a fire in live electrical equipment?",
 "Carbon dioxide (CO2)", ["Water jet", "Foam spray", "Wet chemical"],
 "CO2 does not conduct electricity and leaves no residue. Water jets and foam conduct current back to the user, and wet chemical is made for cooking oil fires."),
("A water extinguisher is best for fires in:",
 "Wood, paper and cloth", ["Petrol and diesel", "Live electrical wiring", "Cooking gas cylinders"],
 "Water cools burning solids such as wood, paper and textiles. On petrol it spreads the fire, and on live wiring it can electrocute the user."),
("When using a portable extinguisher, aim the nozzle at:",
 "The base of the fire", ["The top of the flames", "The smoke above the fire", "The wall behind the fire"],
 "The fuel burns at the base, so that is where the extinguishing agent must go. Spraying the flames above wastes it."),
("In the PASS method for an extinguisher, the first step is to:",
 "Pull the safety pin", ["Press the handle", "Point at the smoke", "Shake the cylinder"],
 "PASS stands for Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze the handle and Sweep from side to side."),
("Why should you crawl low when escaping through a smoke-filled room?",
 "Cleaner air is near the floor", ["The floor stays coolest longest", "Doors open more easily there", "Rescuers search the floor first"],
 "Hot smoke and toxic gases rise, so the air near the floor is the cleanest and coolest to breathe. Smoke, not flames, kills most fire victims."),
("If your clothes catch fire, you should:",
 "Stop, drop and roll", ["Run to find water", "Fan out the flames", "Pull the clothes off"],
 "Running feeds the flames with air. Stopping, dropping to the ground and rolling smothers them; covering the face protects it."),
("During a fire in a tall building, why should you avoid the lift?",
 "It can fail or open onto the fire", ["It is reserved for the injured", "It moves too slowly for crowds", "It fills with water from sprinklers"],
 "Power can fail and trap people in the lift, and the shaft can fill with smoke or open on the fire floor. Use the stairs."),
("What is the first thing to do if you smell cooking gas in a room?",
 "Open doors and windows", ["Switch on a light to look", "Light a match to find it", "Turn on an electric fan"],
 "Let the gas out and shut the cylinder valve if it is safe to do so. A spark from a switch, match or fan motor can ignite the gas."),
("Running a generator inside a closed room is dangerous mainly because of:",
 "Carbon monoxide poisoning", ["Noise damage to hearing", "Electric shock from cables", "Damage to the generator"],
 "Generator exhaust contains carbon monoxide, a gas with no colour or smell that kills people in their sleep. Keep generators outside, away from windows."),
("The needle on a fire extinguisher's pressure gauge sits in the red zone. What does this mean?",
 "It needs recharging or servicing", ["It is full and ready to use", "It is the wrong type for the room", "It must be kept out of the sun"],
 "A needle in the green zone shows the cylinder is pressurised and ready. In the red zone it is under- or over-pressurised and must be serviced before it can be relied on."),
("Which federal agency coordinates disaster management in Nigeria?",
 "NEMA", ["NESREA", "NIMET", "NIHSA"],
 "The National Emergency Management Agency, set up in 1999, coordinates response to disasters, working with state agencies (SEMAs). NESREA enforces environmental standards and NIMET gives weather forecasts."),
("A minor burn should first be cooled with:",
 "Cool running water", ["Ice cubes on the skin", "Palm oil or butter", "Toothpaste or egg white"],
 "Hold the burn under cool running water for about 20 minutes. Ice damages the skin, and oils and pastes trap heat and raise the risk of infection."),
("For heavy bleeding from a cut on the arm, the first aid step is to:",
 "Press firmly on the wound", ["Wash it under the tap", "Pour salt into the wound", "Leave it open to the air"],
 "Firm direct pressure with a clean pad slows the bleeding. Keep pressing and call for help; add more pads on top if blood soaks through."),
("A recruit gets a nosebleed during drill. What is the right first aid?",
 "Lean forward and pinch the soft nose", ["Tilt the head back and look up", "Lie flat on the back at once", "Blow the nose hard to clear it"],
 "Sit the person down, lean the head forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for about 10 minutes, breathing through the mouth. Tilting back or lying flat lets blood run down the throat."),
("In adult CPR, how many chest compressions are given before two rescue breaths?",
 "30", ["15", "20", "5"],
 "The adult ratio is 30 compressions to 2 breaths, at about 100 to 120 compressions a minute. 15 to 2 is used for children by two trained rescuers."),
("During a fire drill, after leaving the building everyone should go to:",
 "The assembly point", ["Their parked cars", "The nearest exit door", "The reception desk"],
 "At the assembly point staff can be counted, so rescuers know at once whether anyone is still inside. Standing at the exits blocks firefighters."),
("Why should you never drive or walk through moving flood water?",
 "It can sweep you away", ["It damages tyre treads", "It is always too cold", "It soaks your documents"],
 "Fast water only knee deep can knock a person down and float a car. Water also hides open drains and washed-out roads."),
]
